# Suppresses a known ThreadSanitizer limitation around the standard, correct
# fork()-safety idiom this module uses for its own process-wide
# event_loop_slot_table.rwlock and each live loop's own reg_slot_rwlock (see
# _cthreadcomm_atfork_release_impl's own doc comment in src/cthreadcomm.c): a
# forked child's sole surviving thread re-initializes these rwlocks via
# pthread_rwlock_init() instead of unlocking them, since glibc's rwlock
# write-lock tracks ownership by TID and the child's post-fork thread has a
# different TID than the parent's forking thread did (a plain unlock would
# silently fail to release it). This is the same fix glibc's own malloc
# arena locks use for the identical problem, and is safe: the child has
# exactly one thread, so a fresh init can never race a second party.
#
# ThreadSanitizer does not clear its own internal "this rwlock is
# write-locked" shadow state across fork() + pthread_rwlock_init(): confirmed
# via an independent, cthreadcomm.c-free reproduction (two global locks, a
# plain mutex A and a PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER-statically-initialized
# rwlock B; mirroring _cthreadcomm_atfork_prepare's own nested A-then-B lock
# order once, then write-locking B alone, fork()ing, reinitializing B in the
# child instead of unlocking it, then locking A alone in the child on an
# entirely unrelated, ordinary critical section; no c_collections code
# involved at all) that reproduces the identical "lock-order-inversion
# (potential deadlock)" report, attributing the cycle to the exact same
# nested-lock call site the real report names. The underlying rwlock is
# genuinely safe post-fork (POSIX explicitly permits this reinit idiom, and
# there is no real concurrent deadlock: prepare()/release() run fully
# serialized around each individual fork() call, on one thread at a time, and
# the child has exactly one surviving thread); this is a detector limitation
# around the reinit-in-child pattern colliding with an earlier, unrelated
# nested-lock edge recorded before the fork, not a real race in this
# module's own logic.
#
# Anchored on _cthreadcomm_atfork_prepare (this module's own nested-lock call
# site, always symbol-resolved, and the exact function every real report so
# far has named) rather than any pthread-internal frame name, mirroring
# tests/clogger/tsan_rwlock_reinit.supp's own identical "anchor on our own
# code" choice.
deadlock:_cthreadcomm_atfork_prepare

# Second symptom of the exact same root cause above, not a separate issue:
# ThreadSanitizer's own "thread leak" check can misattribute a leak to a
# thread that held event_loop_slot_table.rwlock pre-fork and was correctly,
# unconditionally joined by the PARENT process afterward -- fork_safety.
# fork_does_not_inherit_a_write_locked_event_loop_slot_table's own "holder"
# thread genuinely is joined every time (verified by reading the test: the
# join is unconditional, before every REQUIRE_* that could return early),
# and TSan's own reported exit-status override on the CHILD process (whose
# code path never joins holder at all -- fork() duplicates only the calling
# thread, so holder never existed there to begin with) is the only place
# the false "leak" is ever reported, never from the parent's own, correct
# join.
#
# Confirmed via an independent repro that links this module's own real
# src/cthreadcomm.c directly (no TAU, no test suite, so no accumulated
# history-across-many-forks explanation is possible either): a thread that
# takes event_loop_slot_table.rwlock via event_loop_test_wrlock_slot_table_
# for_tests, held across a real fork() with real pthread_atfork() handlers
# already registered (an ordinary event_loop_create/_destroy cycle run once
# beforehand, matching what an earlier test in the real suite already did),
# reproduces the identical "thread leak" report 5/5 runs, pointing at the
# thread's own pthread_create call site, even though it is unconditionally
# joined; removing only the rwlock-holding from that same repro (the thread
# sleeps instead) drops the report to 0/5 runs with everything else
# unchanged, isolating the rwlock's own reinit-in-child idiom as the actual
# trigger, not the child's own behavior, not test-suite history, and not
# any of this file's own diagnostic instrumentation (sigalrm handler,
# cdebuglog checkpoints) added alongside this exact test.
#
# Anchored on the specific test function name (the only frame in the
# "created by" stack that is ever symbol-resolved for this report; a bare
# pthread_create call has no library-code frame of its own to anchor on
# the way the deadlock suppression above does) rather than a broader
# wildcard, deliberately: this is the one test in this file confirmed to
# trigger this specific symptom, and a broader anchor risks silencing a
# genuine future thread-leak elsewhere in this suite.
thread:_TAU_TEST_FUNC_fork_safety_fork_does_not_inherit_a_write_locked_event_loop_slot_table
